# C1 (Windows CI): every *.sh, *.py, and other text file this project ships
# must check out with LF line endings on every platform, including
# windows-latest. Without this file, Windows' default `core.autocrlf=true`
# silently converts LF -> CRLF on checkout for anything Git guesses is
# text. A trailing \r embedded in every line of a bash script is a classic,
# well-known cause of exactly the class of failure this round exists to
# fix: commands that look identical to a human diff but fail at runtime
# with confusing, environment-specific errors (a shebang line ending in
# "bash\r" fails to exec with "No such file or directory"; a value read
# from a subshell or `read` picks up a stray \r as part of the last field
# on the line, corrupting comparisons and paths built from it). This is the
# most plausible, well-documented explanation for the "exit 127" windows-
# latest failures reported against tests/test-config.sh alongside the
# path-separator issue (C1) -- see fix-round-A-report.md for the full
# reasoning and why it could not be verified without Windows CI.
#
# text=auto lets Git still handle genuinely binary files correctly; the
# explicit eol=lf overrides below are the actual fix for the file types
# this project's tooling cares about.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh  text eol=lf
*.py  text eol=lf
*.md  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf

# Never a text file; leave Git's binary detection alone.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
